  "description": [{"value": "TWAIN, MARK (pseud.; real name – Samuel Langhorne Clemens) (1835–1910), American writer. Born November 30, 1835, in the village of Florida (Missouri). He spent his childhood in the town of Hannibal on the Mississippi. He was an apprentice typesetter, and later, together with his brother, published a newspaper in Hannibal, then in Mescatine and Keokuk (Iowa). In 1857, he became an apprentice pilot, realizing his childhood dream of “knowing the river,” and in April 1859 he received his pilot’s license.", "raw": "truncated", "lang": "en", "via": "article first paragraph (machine-translated capture)"}],
